description:
crate-jmap-server/src/parse.rs:223-243 — json_pointer_ext_inner walks JSON Pointer paths via direct byte-index slicing:

  if !path.starts_with('/') {
      return None;
  }

  // Split off the first token.
  let after_slash = &path[1..];
  let (token, remaining) = match after_slash.find('/') {
      Some(pos) => (&after_slash[..pos], &after_slash[pos..]),
      None => (after_slash, ""),
  };

The `&path[1..]` immediately after `starts_with('/')` is the classic "check-then-slice" anti-pattern that `strip_prefix` exists to replace:

  let Some(after_slash) = path.strip_prefix('/') else {
      return None;
  };
  let (token, remaining) = match after_slash.find('/') {
      Some(pos) => (&after_slash[..pos], &after_slash[pos..]),
      None => (after_slash, ""),
  };

Both shapes work and produce identical results — `'/'` is ASCII so the byte index is safe. The reason a serious Rust practitioner prefers `strip_prefix`:

  1. The intent is communicated at the type level. `strip_prefix` returns `Option<&str>` and lets the compiler enforce "we only proceed if the prefix was present". The `starts_with(...) ; &path[1..]` shape says the same thing in two operations, with the slicing implicitly relying on the prior check.
  2. The slice `&path[1..]` is only safe-by-byte-index because `'/'` happens to be one ASCII byte. If a future contributor changes the prefix character to something multi-byte (unlikely here but the pattern propagates), the byte-index assumption silently breaks. `strip_prefix('/')` and `strip_prefix("/")` are both char-aware.
  3. Avoids a clippy::manual_strip lint flag in CI configurations that enable extended lint groups.

The sibling `find('/')` returning a byte position and slicing on `[..pos]` / `[pos..]` is correct and idiomatic for the rest of the function (because `pos` is the byte position of an ASCII `/`). Only the initial prefix-strip is the anti-pattern.

Clippy-class P2 chore. Fix is two-line:

  -    if !path.starts_with('/') {
  -        return None;
  -    }
  -    let after_slash = &path[1..];
  +    let Some(after_slash) = path.strip_prefix('/') else {
  +        return None;
  +    };

Existing tests cover prefix-missing (`json_pointer_ext` with paths missing the leading `/`) and the full ResultReference round-trip; no test changes required.
